IMPORT prelude

-- Health Practitioner Regulation (Adoption of National Law) Act 2009 (NSW) -- the shared rules of the NSW complaints
-- and discipline scheme that the Act puts into the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (NSW).
--
-- WHAT THE ACT DOES. s 4 applies the National Law (the Schedule to the Queensland Act) in NSW "with the modifications
-- set out in Schedule 1"; s 6 declares that NSW does not take part in the national health, performance and conduct
-- process (Part 8, Divisions 3-12 of the National Law); Schedule 1 item [15] puts in its place the NSW Part 8: complaints
-- go to a Council or the Health Care Complaints Commission and are heard by a Professional Standards Committee, an
-- Assessment Committee, a Council at a meeting, or the Civil and Administrative Tribunal. Only the text deposited here
-- (the Act and its Schedule 1, which quote the NSW provisions in full) is encoded; the National Law provisions that
-- Schedule 1 leaves unmodified (the Queensland Schedule) are not deposited and are not encoded.
--
-- This module holds the definitions and rules that both seats use: ss 139, 139B-139E (competence, unsatisfactory
-- professional conduct, professional misconduct), the regulative rules for the 30-day outcome notice (s 145BA(2)) and
-- the 28-day appeal periods (ss 158A(2)(a), 161), the 14 days' notice of a Professional Standards Committee inquiry
-- (s 171(4)), the maximum fines (ss 146C, 148F, 149B), representation (ss 147E, 148D(6), 165J, 171B), appeals against
-- a Committee (s 158) and costs (Schedule 5D cl 13). It exports nothing: the seats' questions are in hpr-seat.l4 (the
-- person who complains) and hpr-seat-practitioner.l4 (the practitioner complained about).
--
-- ANSWERS. JUST TRUE; JUST FALSE where the text says no in its own words ("means", "only if", "unless", "not to be
-- imposed", "does not apply", "not entitled", a period it fixes); NOTHING where it does not answer, with a NOTHING-kind
-- comment. Judgments the Act leaves to a Council, the Commission, a Committee or the Tribunal are inputs.
--
-- Dates use the built-in DATE_SERIAL and DATE_FROM_SERIAL only, not the daydate library.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (NSW) -- shared rules`

§§ `The nouns`

-- The health profession the practitioner is registered in. Medical and nursing and midwifery practitioners go to a
-- Professional Standards Committee (ss 146, 168); the others to an Assessment Committee or a Council meeting (ss 147,
-- 148). Pharmacists and medical practitioners have extra heads of unsatisfactory professional conduct (ss 139C, 139D).
DECLARE `Health profession` IS ONE OF
    `medical`
    `nursing and midwifery`
    `pharmacy`
    `another registered health profession`

-- The body dealing with the complaint (ss 138, 145B(1)(c)-(e)).
DECLARE `Complaint body` IS ONE OF
    `the Tribunal`
    `a Professional Standards Committee`
    `an Assessment Committee`
    `the Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting`

-- The two parties to a complaint.
DECLARE `Complaint party` IS ONE OF
    `the complainant`
    `the practitioner`

-- ss 139B(1), 139C, 139D: the heads of unsatisfactory professional conduct. One answer: the first that fits.
DECLARE `Conduct complained of` IS ONE OF
    `care, skill or judgment significantly below the standard reasonably expected of an equivalent practitioner`
    `a contravention of the National Law or its regulations or the NSW regulations`
    `a contravention of a condition of registration or an undertaking to a National Board`
    `a contravention of a decision or order of a Committee or the Tribunal`
    `a contravention of section 21A(3), 34A(4) or 63G(4) of the Health Care Complaints Act 1993`
    `accepting a benefit for referring or recommending someone to a health service provider`
    `accepting a benefit for recommending a health product, other than ordinary retail conduct`
    `offering or giving a benefit for a referral or recommendation to the practitioner`
    `referring or recommending while having a financial interest in it`
    `overservicing: a service that was unnecessary, not reasonably required or excessive`
    `letting an unregistered assistant attend, treat or operate on patients in matters needing professional skill`
    `other improper or unethical conduct relating to practice`
    `a doctor convicted of an offence listed in s 139C(a)`
    `a doctor knowingly enabling an unregistered person to operate or to act as a doctor`
    `a doctor refusing or failing without reasonable cause to attend a person believed to need urgent attention`
    `a pharmacist practising for pay employed by or in association with a non-pharmacist`
    `a pharmacist supplying precursor drugs unnecessarily or excessively`
    `a pharmacy owner not displaying the owner's name at the main entrance`
    `a pharmacist in charge not displaying their name and "PHARMACIST IN CHARGE"`
    `a pharmacy owner displaying price information that contravenes the Price Information Code`
    `none of these`

-- s 139B(2): the kind of provider or supplier the referral or recommendation was to.
DECLARE `Kind of provider` IS ONE OF
    `a public company`
    `a private company`
    `a natural person`
    `another kind of provider or supplier`

-- What the body found, or the practitioner admitted in writing (ss 146A, 149).
DECLARE `What was found` IS ONE OF
    `unsatisfactory professional conduct`
    `professional misconduct`
    `another ground (competence, impairment, a conviction, suitability)`
    `nothing proved or admitted`

-- ss 147E, 148D(6), 165J, 171B.
DECLARE `Representation` IS ONE OF
    `entitled to attend and to be represented by an Australian legal practitioner, or by another adviser with leave`
    `not entitled to be legally represented`
    `not entitled to be legally represented, but may be accompanied by a support person, who can be an Australian lawyer`
    `the Act gives no right to attend or to be represented`

-- s 161 (and s 158A(2)(a)).
DECLARE `Appeal timing` IS ONE OF
    `in time: within 28 days`
    `in time only because the Tribunal allowed a longer period`
    `out of time, unless the Tribunal allows a longer period`
    `the 28 days have not begun: no written notice of the reasons had been given`

§§ `s 139 -- competent`

-- s 139: "A person is competent to practise a health profession only if the person (a) has sufficient physical
-- capacity, mental capacity, knowledge and skill ...; and (b) has sufficient communication skills ..., including an
-- adequate command of the English language." Each "sufficient" is the deciding body's judgment, asked. All met is read
-- as competent (fork F1: "only if" states necessary conditions).
GIVEN `sufficient physical capacity` IS A BOOLEAN
      `sufficient mental capacity` IS A BOOLEAN
      `sufficient knowledge and skill` IS A BOOLEAN
      `sufficient communication skills, including English` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`competent to practise` MEANS
    IF     `sufficient physical capacity`
       AND `sufficient mental capacity`
       AND `sufficient knowledge and skill`
       AND `sufficient communication skills, including English`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 139B(2) -- financial interest`

-- s 139B(2): a practitioner "has a financial interest in giving a referral or recommendation (a) if the [provider or
-- supplier] is a public company and the practitioner holds 5% or more of the issued share capital ...; (b) if [it] is a
-- private company and the practitioner has any interest in the company; (c) if [it] is a natural person who is a
-- partner of the practitioner; or (d) in any circumstances prescribed by the NSW regulations." The NSW regulations are
-- not deposited: (d) is asked. None of (a)-(d): JUST FALSE (fork F2).
GIVEN `the provider` IS A `Kind of provider`
      `percentage of issued shares held` IS A NUMBER
      `has any interest in the private company` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the provider is the practitioner's partner` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a prescribed circumstance applies` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`has a financial interest` MEANS
    IF `a prescribed circumstance applies`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE CONSIDER `the provider`
         WHEN `a public company` THEN JUST (`percentage of issued shares held` AT LEAST 5)
         WHEN `a private company` THEN JUST `has any interest in the private company`
         WHEN `a natural person` THEN JUST `the provider is the practitioner's partner`
         WHEN `another kind of provider or supplier` THEN JUST FALSE

§§ `ss 139B-139D -- unsatisfactory professional conduct`

GIVEN `the profession` IS A `Health profession`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a medical practitioner` IF `the profession` EQUALS `medical`

GIVEN `the profession` IS A `Health profession`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a pharmacist` IF `the profession` EQUALS `pharmacy`

-- s 139B(1): unsatisfactory professional conduct "includes each of the following"; ss 139C and 139D add heads for
-- medical practitioners and pharmacists. The list is open ("includes"), so conduct that meets no head is NOTHING, never
-- JUST FALSE: s 139B(1)(l) ("any other improper or unethical conduct") is the deciding body's catch-all.
-- (i) applies "unless the practitioner discloses the nature of the interest to the person before or at the time";
-- s 139C(c) "unless the practitioner has taken all reasonable steps to ensure that another medical practitioner attends".
GIVEN `the profession` IS A `Health profession`
      `the conduct` IS A `Conduct complained of`
      `financial interest` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `disclosed the interest before or at the time` IS A BOOLEAN
      `took all reasonable steps to have another doctor attend` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`is unsatisfactory professional conduct` MEANS
    IF `meets a head`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind: "includes" list; the conduct meets no listed head, and s 139B(1)(l) is the deciding body's judgment
    WHERE
        `meets a head` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the conduct`
            WHEN `referring or recommending while having a financial interest in it` THEN
                    `financial interest` EQUALS JUST TRUE
                AND (NOT `disclosed the interest before or at the time`)
            WHEN `a doctor convicted of an offence listed in s 139C(a)` THEN `is a medical practitioner` `the profession`
            WHEN `a doctor knowingly enabling an unregistered person to operate or to act as a doctor` THEN `is a medical practitioner` `the profession`
            WHEN `a doctor refusing or failing without reasonable cause to attend a person believed to need urgent attention` THEN
                    `is a medical practitioner` `the profession`
                AND (NOT `took all reasonable steps to have another doctor attend`)
            WHEN `a pharmacist practising for pay employed by or in association with a non-pharmacist` THEN `is a pharmacist` `the profession`
            WHEN `a pharmacist supplying precursor drugs unnecessarily or excessively` THEN `is a pharmacist` `the profession`
            WHEN `a pharmacy owner not displaying the owner's name at the main entrance` THEN `is a pharmacist` `the profession`
            WHEN `a pharmacist in charge not displaying their name and "PHARMACIST IN CHARGE"` THEN `is a pharmacist` `the profession`
            WHEN `a pharmacy owner displaying price information that contravenes the Price Information Code` THEN `is a pharmacist` `the profession`
            WHEN `none of these` THEN FALSE
            OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `s 139E -- professional misconduct`

-- s 139E: professional misconduct "means (a) unsatisfactory professional conduct of a sufficiently serious nature to
-- justify suspension or cancellation of the practitioner's registration; or (b) more than one instance of unsatisfactory
-- professional conduct that, when ... considered together, amount to conduct of [that] nature". "Sufficiently serious"
-- is the Tribunal's judgment, asked. A "means" definition: unsatisfactory professional conduct that is not serious
-- enough is JUST FALSE; where unsatisfactory professional conduct is itself not established (NOTHING), so is this.
GIVEN `unsatisfactory professional conduct` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `serious enough to justify suspension or cancellation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`is professional misconduct` MEANS
    CONSIDER `unsatisfactory professional conduct`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST `serious enough to justify suspension or cancellation`
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind: the conduct is not shown to be unsatisfactory professional conduct (open list)

§§ `The regulative rules`

DECLARE `Scheme party` IS ONE OF
    `the Council`
    `the appellant`

DECLARE `Scheme act` IS ONE OF
    `give the complainant written notice of the outcome`
    `lodge the appeal with the Tribunal`

-- The periods the three rules fix, each named once: the rules take them after WITHIN, and the last days below are
-- worked from the same names, so a date and its rule cannot drift apart.
-- s 145BA(2): "within 30 days after the outcome".
`the s 145BA(2) period` MEANS 30
-- s 161(a): "within 28 days after the day the person making the appeal was given written notice of the reasons".
`the s 161 period` MEANS 28
-- s 158A(2)(a): "within 28 days after the date of the Committee's decision on the point of law".
`the s 158A(2)(a) period` MEANS 28

-- s 145BA(2): "The Council must give the complainant notice in writing of a notifiable outcome within 30 days after the
-- outcome." Counted in days from the outcome (day 0); notice on day 30 is in time.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Scheme party` `Scheme act`
`s 145BA(2) -- the Council must tell the complainant the outcome within 30 days` MEANS
    PARTY `the Council`
    MUST `give the complainant written notice of the outcome`
    WITHIN `the s 145BA(2) period`
    LEST BREACH BY `the Council` BECAUSE "no written notice of the notifiable outcome within 30 days after it (s 145BA(2))"

-- s 161: an appeal (other than on a point of law) "must be made (a) within 28 days after the day the person making the
-- appeal was given written notice of the reasons for the decision ...; or (b) within the longer period allowed by the
-- Tribunal". Counted in days from the day of the notice (day 0); an appeal on day 28 is in time. Jurally a right that
-- lapses; the rule models the lapse as LEST BREACH.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Scheme party` `Scheme act`
`s 161 -- an appeal must be made within 28 days after written notice of the reasons` MEANS
    PARTY `the appellant`
    MUST `lodge the appeal with the Tribunal`
    WITHIN `the s 161 period`
    LEST BREACH BY `the appellant` BECAUSE "no appeal within 28 days after written notice of the reasons, and no longer period allowed (s 161)"

-- s 158A(2)(a): an appeal on a point of law during a Committee inquiry may be made "within 28 days after the date of the
-- Committee's decision on the point of law". Counted from the date of the decision (day 0).
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Scheme party` `Scheme act`
`s 158A(2)(a) -- a point of law appeal during an inquiry must be made within 28 days of the decision` MEANS
    PARTY `the appellant`
    MUST `lodge the appeal with the Tribunal`
    WITHIN `the s 158A(2)(a) period`
    LEST BREACH BY `the appellant` BECAUSE "no point of law appeal within 28 days after the Committee's decision on the point (s 158A(2)(a))"

GIVEN `days after the outcome` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `outcome notice given in time` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 145BA(2) -- the Council must tell the complainant the outcome within 30 days` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `days after the outcome`), (EVENT `the Council` `give the complainant written notice of the outcome` `days after the outcome`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `days after the notice of reasons` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `appeal lodged within the s 161 period` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 161 -- an appeal must be made within 28 days after written notice of the reasons` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `days after the notice of reasons`), (EVENT `the appellant` `lodge the appeal with the Tribunal` `days after the notice of reasons`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `days after the decision on the point` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `point of law appeal lodged within 28 days` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 158A(2)(a) -- a point of law appeal during an inquiry must be made within 28 days of the decision` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `days after the decision on the point`), (EVENT `the appellant` `lodge the appeal with the Tribunal` `days after the decision on the point`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `s 161 -- when an appeal must be made`

GIVEN `the day I was given written notice of the reasons` IS A DATE
      `the day I lodged the appeal` IS A DATE
      `the Tribunal allowed a longer period` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Appeal timing`
`timing of an appeal` MEANS
    IF `the days` LESS THAN 0
    THEN `the 28 days have not begun: no written notice of the reasons had been given`
    ELSE IF `appeal lodged within the s 161 period` `the days`
         THEN `in time: within 28 days`
         ELSE IF `the Tribunal allowed a longer period`
              THEN `in time only because the Tribunal allowed a longer period`
              ELSE `out of time, unless the Tribunal allows a longer period`
    WHERE
        `the days` MEANS DATE_SERIAL `the day I lodged the appeal` MINUS DATE_SERIAL `the day I was given written notice of the reasons`

-- The last day for an appeal under s 161(a): the end of the period the s 161 rule runs (28 days after the day of the
-- notice).
GIVEN `the day I was given written notice of the reasons` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`last day to appeal` MEANS DATE_FROM_SERIAL (DATE_SERIAL `the day I was given written notice of the reasons` PLUS `the s 161 period`)

-- The last day for notice of a notifiable outcome: the end of the period the s 145BA(2) rule runs (30 days after the
-- outcome).
GIVEN `the day of the outcome` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`last day to give notice of the outcome` MEANS DATE_FROM_SERIAL (DATE_SERIAL `the day of the outcome` PLUS `the s 145BA(2) period`)

§§ `s 158A(2) -- appeals on a point of law to the Tribunal`

DECLARE `Stage of the Committee inquiry` IS ONE OF
    `before notice of the inquiry was given`
    `after notice of the inquiry, before it began`
    `during the inquiry`
    `after the inquiry was completed`

-- s 158A(2): "An appeal may be made (a) during an inquiry -- within 28 days after the date of the Committee's decision on
-- the point of law ...; or (b) before the commencement of an inquiry but after the date of giving notice of the inquiry."
-- Outside (a) and (b) the text does not say an appeal may not be made (no "only"): NOTHING. s 161 does not reach a
-- point of law appeal (it excepts them), so after the inquiry no period is given at all (finding 4).
GIVEN `the stage` IS A `Stage of the Committee inquiry`
      `days after the Committee's decision on the point` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`point of law appeal may be made` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the stage`
    WHEN `after notice of the inquiry, before it began` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `during the inquiry` THEN JUST (`point of law appeal lodged within 28 days` `days after the Committee's decision on the point`)
    WHEN `before notice of the inquiry was given` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind: scope limit with no "only"; s 158A(2) does not cover this stage
    WHEN `after the inquiry was completed` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind: s 158A(2) gives no period after the inquiry, and s 161 excepts points of law

§§ `s 171(4) -- notice of a Professional Standards Committee inquiry`

-- s 171(4): "The Chairperson must give not less than 14 days' notice of the inquiry to (a) the relevant health
-- practitioner; and (b) the complainant, if any; ...". Read as 14 clear days: neither the day of the notice nor the day
-- of the inquiry counts (fork F3).
GIVEN `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE
      `the day of the inquiry` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`given enough notice of the inquiry` MEANS
    JUST ((DATE_SERIAL `the day of the inquiry` MINUS DATE_SERIAL `the day the notice was given` MINUS 1) AT LEAST 14)

-- The earliest day the inquiry may be held after a notice given on a day: 15 days later (14 clear days between).
GIVEN `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`earliest day of the inquiry` MEANS DATE_FROM_SERIAL (DATE_SERIAL `the day the notice was given` PLUS 15)

§§ `ss 146C, 148F, 149B -- the maximum fine`

GIVEN `the profession` IS A `Health profession`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a relevant health practitioner for a Professional Standards Committee` IF
    `the profession` EQUALS `medical` OR `the profession` EQUALS `nursing and midwifery`

-- The cap in penalty units: 50 for a Professional Standards Committee (s 146C(1)) and for a Council at a meeting
-- (s 148F(1)); 250 for the Tribunal (s 149B(1)). An Assessment Committee has no power to fine (it reports and
-- recommends, s 147D): NOTHING.
GIVEN `the body` IS A `Complaint body`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`fine cap in penalty units` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the body`
    WHEN `the Tribunal` THEN JUST 250
    WHEN `a Professional Standards Committee` THEN JUST 50
    WHEN `the Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting` THEN JUST 50
    WHEN `an Assessment Committee` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind: the Act gives an Assessment Committee no power to fine

-- ss 146A, 146C, 148, 148F, 149, 149B. A fine "is not to be imposed unless (a) the [body] finds the [practitioner] to
-- have been guilty of unsatisfactory professional conduct [the Tribunal: or professional misconduct]; and (b) the
-- [body] is satisfied there is no other order, or combination of orders, that is appropriate in the public interest",
-- and "is not to be imposed if a fine or other penalty has already been imposed by a court in respect of the conduct".
-- A Professional Standards Committee's powers reach only medical and nursing and midwifery practitioners (s 146); a
-- Council meeting's do not reach them (s 148). Where the text bars a fine, the answer is JUST 0. Professional
-- misconduct is unsatisfactory professional conduct of a serious kind (s 139E), so it meets ss 146C(2)(a) and 148F(2)(a)
-- too (fork F4). The answer is in dollars: the cap in penalty units times the value of a penalty unit (set outside
-- this Act, by s 17 of the Crimes (Sentencing Procedure) Act 1999, not deposited; asked).
GIVEN `the profession` IS A `Health profession`
      `the body` IS A `Complaint body`
      `the finding` IS A `What was found`
      `no other order is appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a court has already fined or penalised the conduct` IS A BOOLEAN
      `dollars per penalty unit` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`maximum fine in dollars` MEANS
    CONSIDER `fine cap in penalty units` `the body`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind: no fine power for this body in the Act
    WHEN JUST `cap` THEN
        IF `the fine is barred`
        THEN JUST 0
        ELSE JUST (`cap` TIMES `dollars per penalty unit`)
    WHERE
        `the fine is barred` MEANS
               `a court has already fined or penalised the conduct`
            OR (NOT `no other order is appropriate`)
            OR (NOT (`the finding` EQUALS `unsatisfactory professional conduct` OR `the finding` EQUALS `professional misconduct`))
            OR (`the body` EQUALS `a Professional Standards Committee` AND (NOT (`is a relevant health practitioner for a Professional Standards Committee` `the profession`)))
            OR (`the body` EQUALS `the Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting` AND `is a relevant health practitioner for a Professional Standards Committee` `the profession`)

§§ `ss 147E, 148D(6), 165J, 171B -- representation`

-- s 165J(1) (the Tribunal) and s 171B(1) (a Professional Standards Committee): the practitioner "and any complainant
-- concerned are entitled to attend and to be represented by (a) an Australian legal practitioner; or (b) with the leave
-- of the [presiding member or Chairperson], another adviser". s 147E (an Assessment Committee): "A complainant and the
-- relevant health practitioner ... are not entitled to be legally represented". s 148D(1), (6) (a Council meeting): the
-- practitioner "is entitled to attend ... and to make submissions" and "is not entitled to be legally represented ...
-- but may be accompanied by a support person. The support person can be an Australian lawyer." Subdivision 5 gives the
-- complainant no right to attend a Council meeting at all (finding 5).
GIVEN `the body` IS A `Complaint body`
      `the party` IS A `Complaint party`
GIVETH A `Representation`
`representation before the body` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the body`
    WHEN `the Tribunal` THEN `entitled to attend and to be represented by an Australian legal practitioner, or by another adviser with leave`
    WHEN `a Professional Standards Committee` THEN `entitled to attend and to be represented by an Australian legal practitioner, or by another adviser with leave`
    WHEN `an Assessment Committee` THEN `not entitled to be legally represented`
    WHEN `the Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting` THEN
        IF `the party` EQUALS `the practitioner`
        THEN `not entitled to be legally represented, but may be accompanied by a support person, who can be an Australian lawyer`
        ELSE `the Act gives no right to attend or to be represented`

§§ `s 158 -- appeals against a Committee`

-- s 158(1): "If a complaint ... is referred to a Committee, the practitioner or student or the complainant, may appeal
-- against any of the following to the Tribunal -- (a) a finding of the Committee; (b) the exercise of a power by the
-- Committee under Subdivision 3 of Division 3; (c) the exercise by the Tribunal List Manager of a power under that
-- Subdivision." "Committee" means an Assessment Committee or a Professional Standards Committee (s 138). Against the
-- Tribunal or a Council meeting, s 158 says nothing: NOTHING (the Council's decisions are appealed by the practitioner
-- under s 159; the Tribunal's under the Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013, not deposited).
GIVEN `the body` IS A `Complaint body`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`may appeal against the Committee` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the body`
    WHEN `a Professional Standards Committee` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `an Assessment Committee` THEN JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind: s 158 covers Committees only; other routes are outside this deposit

§§ `Schedule 5D cl 13 -- costs`

-- Schedule 5D cl 13(1): "The Tribunal may order the complainant (if any), the registered health practitioner or student
-- concerned, or any other person entitled to appear ... at an inquiry or appeal before the Tribunal to pay costs to
-- another person as decided by the Tribunal." Nothing in the deposit lets a Committee or a Council award costs: NOTHING.
GIVEN `the body` IS A `Complaint body`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`may be ordered to pay costs` MEANS
    IF `the body` EQUALS `the Tribunal`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind: the Act is silent on costs before a Committee or a Council
